test("createUser saves to database", async () => {
  await createUser({ name: "Alice" });
  const row = await db.query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = ?", ["Alice"]);
  expect(row).toBeDefined();
});

// GOOD: Verifies through interface
test("createUser makes user retrievable", async () => {
  const user = await createUser({ name: "Alice" });
  const retrieved = await getUser(user.id);
  expect(retrieved.name).toBe("Alice");
});
```

**Tautological tests**: Expected value restates the implementation, so the test passes by construction.

```typescript
// BAD: Expected value is recomputed the way the code computes it
test("calculateTotal sums line items", () => {
  const items = [{ price: 10 }, { price: 5 }];
  const expected = items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
  expect(calculateTotal(items)).toBe(expected);
});

// GOOD: Expected value is an independent, known literal
test("calculateTotal sums line items", () => {
  expect(calculateTotal([{ price: 10 }, { price: 5 }])).toBe(15);
});
```

# [BUG] {Short description of the incorrect behavior}

## Severity

**{Critical / High / Medium / Low}** — {Brief justification: e.g. "blocks the payment flow in production" / "affects mobile layout but has a workaround"}

---

## Current Behavior

{What happens today. Be specific and objective. Include evidence when possible:}

- Screenshot: {attach or describe}
- Log / exact error: `{paste the error or write N/A}`
- Affected URL or route: `{e.g. /dashboard/settings}`

---

## Expected Behavior

{What should happen according to the design or business logic. Describe the correct state of the system.}

---

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Go to {URL or section}
2. {Specific action: e.g. "Click the 'Save' button"}
3. {Observe: what happens that shouldn't}

**Frequency:** {Always / Sometimes / Rarely / Could not reproduce locally}

---

## Environment

- **Browser / OS / Device:** {e.g. Chrome 124 / macOS 14 / iPhone 15}
- **Affected environment:** {Production / Staging / Local}
- **Build version:** {e.g. v2.3.1 or commit hash if applicable}

---

## User Impact *(optional)*

- **Affected users:** {Approximate number or percentage, if known}
- **Workaround available:** {Yes — describe it / No}

---

## Summary

{Maximum 3 lines: what is broken, where it occurs, and how to confirm the fix worked.}

# [IMP] {Description of the proposed improvement}

## Context

{Context about the feature or section to be improved. Describe the affected area of the product and its current role within the system.}

---

## Origin

- **Requested by:** {Client / area / internal team}
- **Date:** {YYYY-MM-DD}
- **Motivation:** {If internal initiative: what data, friction, or signal motivated this change. If external: reference to the original request (ticket, email, meeting).}

---

## Current State

{How the affected feature works today. Describe the current behavior of the system or interface objectively.}

---

## Requested Change or Improvement

{Precise description of what must be modified or added. Be specific about the expected new behavior.}

{If wireframes, mockups, or reference examples are available, attach or link them here.}

---

## Expected Impact

{What improves with this change: user experience, conversion, operation time, error reduction, etc.}

{Quantify if possible: e.g. "reduces load time from 4s to <1s" / "removes 3 steps from the checkout flow".}

---

## Justification *(required if origin is external)*

{Why this change is necessary. If there is data to support it, include it. What impact would there be if it's not done.}

---

## Validation Hypothesis *(recommended if origin is internal)*

{How we will know this change worked. What metric or signal indicates success. E.g. "Step 2 abandonment rate drops 10% within 30 days".}

---

## Technical Scope *(optional)*

{Modules, services, screens, or components affected. If the technical impact is clear from the description, specify it here.}

---

## Acceptance Criteria *(optional for small improvements)*

- [ ] {Verifiable condition that the change was implemented correctly}
- [ ] {Additional verifiable condition if applicable}

---

## Summary

{Maximum 3 lines: what to change, why it is necessary now, and how to verify that the improvement was implemented.}

# [SPIKE] {Question or unknown to resolve}

## Central Question

{The specific, well-defined question this spike must answer. One question only. E.g. "Can we use the Stripe API for installment payments without modifying our webhook architecture?"}

---

## Context

{Why we need to know this before moving forward. What is blocked. Which decision or task cannot proceed until this question is answered.}

---

## Expected Deliverable

{What must exist at the end of the timebox. Choose one or more:}

- [ ] Technical document with findings and conclusion
- [ ] Functional proof of concept (PoC)
- [ ] Documented decision with justification
- [ ] Informed estimate for the task that requires it

{Describe the expected format of the deliverable: e.g. "a Notion document with an options comparison and recommendation", "a branch with test code".}

---

## Timebox

**{E.g. 4 hours / 1 day / 2 days}** — This time is non-negotiable. If no conclusion is reached within the timebox, document the current state of the analysis and escalate.

---

## Summary

{Maximum 3 lines: what to investigate, why it is blocking, and what will be delivered at the end.}

# [FE/BE/DevOps] {Concrete technical description}

> Replace the prefix with the corresponding area: `[FE]` for frontend, `[BE]` for backend, `[DevOps]` for infrastructure/CI.

---

## Objective

{What is to be achieved technically. Why it is necessary to do this now. Include the business or technical context that justifies this task.}

---

## Technical Description

{Implementation details. Be precise:}

- **Affected architecture:** {modules, layers, involved services}
- **Required changes:** {what must be created, modified, or deleted}
- **Expected behavior after implementation:** {how the system should work once the task is completed}

{Add diagrams, schematics, or reference code snippets if they aid understanding.}

---

## Completion Criteria

{How to verify that the task is done. Must be objectively verifiable:}

- [ ] {Specific test that passes / metric reached / observable validation}
- [ ] {Additional criterion if applicable}

---

## Risks / Considerations *(optional)*

{Possible side effects of this change. Sensitive areas of the code that may be affected. Dependencies to keep in mind.}

---

## Rollback Plan *(optional)*

{Plan in case things go wrong and the change needs to be undone. E.g. "Revert commit X" / "Run migration down" / "Restore feature flag to false".}

---

## Summary

{Maximum 3 lines: what to implement, why it is necessary now, and how to verify it is done.}
